{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And he called for Moses… at night.</b> The reason for its saying the word <b>לַיְלָה</b> a second time: perhaps it is because Pharaoh was apprehensive, in his calling for Moses, that he would not come out to meet him out of fear of his decree which he had declared to him (Exodus 10:28): ״אַל תּוֹסֶף רְאוֹת פָּנַי כִּי בְּיוֹם רְאוֹתְךָ פָנַי תָּמוּת״ — and if so, he would worry lest he impute this against him; therefore he said in his call ״לַיְלָה״ — meaning, this is not under the conditions of the decree, for he had said only by day and not by night, and this time is nighttime. Or, because he would not see his face, he says that it is nighttime, and there is no claim of ״אַל תּוֹסֶף רְאוֹת פָּנַי,״ for it is in darkness — no one sees [the other], and so forth." "<b>Or</b> the verse may intend to state Moses' reply to Pharaoh when he called him, for he answered him and said to him ״לַיְלָה״ — and I cannot go out to speak with you, for HaShem commanded (Exodus 12:22): ״לֹא תֵצְאוּ אִישׁ מִפֶּתַח בֵּיתוֹ עַד בֹּקֶר״." "And Pharaoh said ״<b>Rise up, go out</b>״ and so forth — meaning, he made them free men on their own. Or perhaps he alluded in the word <b>קוּמוּ</b> the language of rising up and lifting the head, after the manner of what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Genesis Rabbah 58:8) on the verse (Genesis 23:17) ״וַיָּקָם שְׂדֵה עֶפְרוֹן.״ And he reckoned that through this the plague would be lifted from him once the matter was completed from his side by sending them off — even if the hindrance should come from them, there is no blame upon him." "<b>Further,</b> he alludes in his saying <b>קוּמוּ צְּאוּ</b>, that since they did not want to come out to him, he called them to rise — for he thought they did not want to rise from their beds to come to him, and he said that his calling them is for their benefit, for he says to them ״צְּאוּ״ and so forth. And according to this approach, the reason for its saying ״לַיְלָה״ is that the verse preceded to state the reason why they did not go out to meet him immediately — and HaShem had commanded concerning the honor of the kingship; therefore it first stated the circumstance that caused their delay from going out, and said ״לַיְלָה.״ And this is the reason they did not rise and go out to him: for HaShem commanded ״לֹא תֵצְאוּ אִישׁ מִפֶּתַח בֵּיתוֹ״ and so forth (Exodus 12:22)." "<b>Also you</b> and so forth. One must know why he said ״גַּם״ at the beginning of the statement? And further, were Moses and Aaron imprisoned, that it was necessary to say ״צְּאוּ מִתּוֹךְ עַמִּי״? Perhaps it is that from within his words he revealed his thinking, for he desired that Moses and Aaron be present; therefore he said <b>גַּם אַתֶּם</b> in addition to Israel. And since the matter is not understood from the word ״גַּם״ — that it refers to Israel — for in truth Moses was not imprisoned, and when he said ״גַּם״ the preceding reference to the children of Israel is not in its meaning, and it would not be fitting to say ״גַּם אַתֶּם״ except according to Pharaoh's thinking as mentioned; therefore he went back to explain <b>גַּם בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל.</b>" "<b>Further</b>, it intends that he wished to drive them out even if they desired of their own will to remain among them. Accordingly, it is justified to say <b>gam atem</b> (״even you״) — even though you are at your own discretion, nevertheless I do not desire you to remain in my land. This is a novel point, and it is what HaShem said (Exodus 11:1): ״כְּשַׁלְּחוֹ כָּלָה גָּרֵשׁ יְגָרֵשׁ אֶתְכֶם״. And His saying <b>gam benei Yisrael</b> comes to include the children and the women, and His saying <b>gam tzonchem</b> etc. comes to include the rest of the livestock, or to include what they had asked him to aid them with."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:35.347539647Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}